 Let $A \sub \VF^n$ be a definable set with $\dim_{\VF}(A) = n$ and $f : A \fun \mdl P(\VF^m)$ a definable finitary function. We say that $f$ is \memph{continuous} if $\lim_{ A \rightarrow a} f = f(a)$ for all $a \in A$.
 
 \begin{lem}\label{fini:conti}
-The function $f$ is continuous almost everywhere.
+Suppose $n>0$. Then $f$ is continuous almost everywhere.
 \end{lem}
 \begin{proof}
 This can be reduced to the familiar case that $f$ is indeed a function into $\VF^m$ as follows. By Lemma~\ref{finite:VF:project:RV} and compactness, there are a definable set $U$ in $\RV$ and a definable function $g: A \times \VF^m \fun A \times U$ such that, for all $a \in A$, $\pr_A(g(a, b)) = a$  and $g \rest (a \times f(a))$ is injective. For each $t \in U$, let
 \[
 A_t = \set{a \in A \given (a,t) \in g(a \times f(a))}
 \]
-and $f_t \sub f$ be the obvious $t$-definable function $A_t \fun \VF^m$. By dimension theory, more precisely, by Lemma~\ref{full:dim:open:poly}, $\dim_{\VF}(A_t) = n$ for some $t \in U$. For such a $t \in U$, by Lemma~\ref{fun:almost:cont}, $f_t$ is continuous almost everywhere.  The claim follows.
+and $f_t \sub f$ be the obvious $t$-definable function $A_t \fun \VF^m$. The discontinuity locus $E$ of $f$ is definable: the limit condition is first-order, and minimality of the finite set $f(a)$ can be tested by removing one of its elements at a time. Suppose for contradiction that $\dim_{\VF}(E)=n$. By Lemma~\ref{full:dim:open:poly}, $E$ contains a nonempty open polydisc. Apply generic local constancy \cite[Lemma~8.9]{Yin:special:trans} to the finite label-set map
+\[
+a\efun g(a\times f(a))_{\RV}.
+\]
+After shrinking to a nonempty open polydisc $P\sub E$, this set is a fixed finite set $T$. If $T$ is empty, then $f$ is the constant empty correspondence on $P$ and is continuous there, a contradiction.
+
+For each $t\in T$, the inverse branch $f_t$ is defined on all of $P$. Extend it by zero outside $A_t$ and apply Lemma~\ref{fun:almost:cont}. The finitely many branch-exception sets have union of $\VF$-dimension less than $n$, so choose $a\in P$ outside their union. All branches are continuous at $a$. Their finite union has limit set $f(a)$; this limit set is minimal because the distinct values at $a$ can be separated by sufficiently small discs and, since $n>0$, the punctured polydisc still accumulates at $a$. Thus $f$ is continuous at $a$, contradicting $P\sub E$. Hence $\dim_{\VF}(E)<n$.
 \end{proof}
 
